items=[('Pretectal restraint','Effective pretectal and caudal thalamic damage releases catching toward large or unsuitable objects; preserved snapping separates this effect from loss of the motor capacity.'),('Selectivity and excitability','Configuration filtering and response strength are distinct: some cuts increase firing without eliminating the worm–antiworm distinction.'),('Timed input interaction','Pretectal conditioning strongly attenuates an early optic-nerve-evoked tectal component at a lead of approximately 17–25 ms; adaptation controls support a pathway-specific effect.'),('Distinct electrical measurements','Unit spikes, EEG and slow extracellular potentials measure different activity; more firing can coexist with a smaller population field.'),('NPY evidence','Local NPY reduces the negative tectal field component, but receptor identity, endogenous release and necessity for avoidance were not established by the application experiment.'),('Behavioral bias and molecular limits','High pretectal DC recruits retreat and crouching while generally reducing approach; glial potassium modulation remains a proposed explanation rather than a measured pathway.')]
